{{Politics of Okaiken}}

The '''Ministry of the Environment''' ({{lang-ja|環境省}}, {{Nihongo|Kankyō-shō|かんきょうしょう}}, abbreviated '''MOE''') is a cabinet-level ministry of the [[Government of Okaiken]] responsible for national environmental protection, industrial pollution abatement, biodiversity conservation, climate change mitigation, and circular economy waste management. Formally elevated to full cabinet status in the 2001 Central Government Reform from the former Environment Agency, the ministry commands an annual state appropriation of approximately 16 billion Sen and directs 11,400 environmental civil servants across its Kasumigaseki headquarters and regional offices.

The ministry oversees the [[Environmental Impact Assessment Agency (Okaiken)|Environmental Impact Assessment Agency]], the [[National Institute for Environmental Studies (Okaiken)|National Institute for Environmental Studies]], and statutory national park reserves. The incumbent Minister of the Environment is [[Yumi Yamada]] of the [[Sanese Communist Party]] (SCP), Member of the House of Commoners for [[Okami Metropolis|Okami]] 25th, who took office on 20 May 2026 within the [[Ikazaki Cabinet]]. In coalition governance, Yamada has led a regulatory crackdown against illegal electronic waste abandonment, enforced rigorous environmental impact audits on commercial data corridors, and expanded public legal remedies for frontline communities afflicted by industrial runoff.

==Historical development and pollution jurisprudence==

===The Four Pollution Diseases and the Environment Agency===

Modern environmental governance in Okaiken was born from popular revolt against the catastrophic industrial pollution of the 1960s, known historically as the Four Pollution Diseases (四大公害病, ''Shi Dai Kōgai-byō''). Severe mercury contamination, cadmium poisoning, and industrial sulfur dioxide emissions generated widespread paralysis, asthma, and fatalities across coastal manufacturing basins. Under immense public outrage, the National Diet enacted the 1970 Pollution Diet legislative package, passing fourteen environmental statutes in a single legislative sitting.

To enforce these protections, the government inaugurated the Environment Agency (環境庁, ''Kankyō-chō'') on 1 July 1971 as an external bureau of the Prime Minister's Office. Although the agency promulgated the landmark Environmental Impact Assessment Act of 1973 and established rigorous national air and water quality ceilings, its subordinate status within the bureaucratic hierarchy frequently left it outmaneuvered by the industrial interests of MITI and the public works apparatus of the Ministry of Construction.

===The 2001 elevation and modern climate governance===

On 6 January 2001, the National Diet enacted the 2001 Central Government Reform, elevating the agency to the full cabinet-level Ministry of the Environment. The reform consolidated nature conservation, national parks administration, and industrial waste management into a single sovereign portfolio. Throughout the 2010s, the ministry's remit expanded into international climate diplomacy, committing the state to carbon neutrality goals and regulating renewable energy transitions.

Under the conservative Honda administration, environmental enforcement was weakened to facilitate corporate digital expansion. Server farms and semiconductor facilities built under the Yagikoshu Data Corridor were granted fast-track environmental waivers, resulting in widespread heavy metal groundwater contamination. Following the May 2026 [[Satori AI Blackout Crisis]], thousands of defunct server racks and battery storage units were unlawfully dumped in rural woodlands. Upon assuming office, Minister Yumi Yamada launched the National E-Waste Remediation Initiative, holding corporate parent entities criminally liable for toxic hazardous dumping under the Waste Management Act.

